course: dictionary
lesson: 13254
title: Where a word tells us where we come from
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you where a person or thing comes from. Glaswegian is one of them. It means connected with Glasgow, the biggest city in Scotland. «Mia was born in Glasgow, so she is a Glaswegian.» The ending -ian often shows a place or its people, as in Italian or Brazilian.
explain: Glaswegian can be a noun or an adjective. As a noun, it names a person: «Two Glaswegians waved from the bus.» As an adjective, it describes something: «The Glaswegian shops were busy on Saturday.» Look at the words next to it to see which job it is doing.
explain: Glaswegian can also name the way people from Glasgow talk. Their accent and dialect have their own words, such as «wean» (child) and «messages» (shopping). If the sentence is about how someone speaks, the word is describing their accent or dialect.
fact: A Glaswegian is a person who comes from Glasgow.
fact: Glasgow is the biggest city in Scotland.
fact: Glaswegian can be a noun (a Glaswegian, Glaswegians) or an adjective (Glaswegian).
fact: As an adjective, Glaswegian means belonging to Glasgow, such as its people, buildings or way of speaking.
fact: Glaswegian is also the strong local accent and dialect of Glasgow, with words like «wean» (child) and «messages» (shopping).
exercise: «Ava is a Glaswegian, so she was born in Glasgow.» What is a Glaswegian? | A kind of bus in London | A river in Wales | A type of cake | *A person who comes from Glasgow
exercise: «The Glaswegian shops were busy on Saturday.» What kind of word is Glaswegian in this sentence? | A full stop | A noun for a river | *An adjective | A verb
exercise: In Glasgow, a child is called a «wean». Which word from Glasgow speech means child? | *wean | messages | glum | castle
test: «Two Glaswegians waved from the bus as it passed the park.» Who are the Glaswegians? | Buses from London | Trees in the park | *People who come from Glasgow | A type of weather
test: «Liam spoke with a Glaswegian accent, and his words sounded warm and lively.» What does Glaswegian describe here? | A kind of food | *The accent of people from Glasgow | A wet and grey day | A city in Spain
exam: «Mum said, "Can you get the messages on the way home?" and Ellie laughed because she knew it meant the shopping.» What does Glaswegian speech use the word messages to mean? | *Shopping | A kind of song | A text on a phone | A letter from school
